Title: "Groups and Subgroups"
Id: "5bc745b6-657d-446f-b5f0-cae8c199112a"


Text: "A *group* is a monoid in which every element possesses a two-sided
       inverse: for each $x \in G$, there exists an element $y \in G$ satisfying
       $x * y = e$ and $y * x = e$.

       Groups constitute the foundational paradigm of modern algebra. While
       monoids can represent irreversible processes (such as non-injective
       functions or lossy data compression), groups formalize the geometry of
       *reversible transformations* and *symmetries*. Historically conceived by
       Évariste Galois in 1832 to characterize the solvability of polynomial
       equations by radicals, groups now permeate every branch of modern
       mathematics and theoretical physics, from crystallography and quantum
       mechanics to gauge theory and algebraic topology."
Id: "5fb80940-d8de-44bf-8117-eeee203d4755"


[\group]
Declares: G ::= (X, *, e) is \monoid via (X, *, e)
satisfies:
. forAll: x "in" G
  then:
  . exists: y "in" G
    suchThat:
    . x * y = e
    . y * x = e
Enables:
. capability: x_ "in" G :<->: x_ is \group.element:of{G}
  written: "x_? \in G?"
Documented:
. called: "group"
. description: "A group is a monoid in which every element $x$ has an inverse
                $y$, so that $x * y = e = y * x$. Equivalently it is an
                associative structure with identity in which every element is
                invertible; this makes it a quasigroup as well as a monoid."
Id: "e4d7371d-29bd-431f-ab09-e114fe89e5cf"


Text: "A group is *finite* when its underlying carrier set $X$ has finite
       cardinality. The cardinality $|G| = |X|$ is called the *order* of the
       group. The order of a finite group exerts immense control over its
       internal substructure: by Lagrange's theorem and the Sylow theorems, the
       arithmetic prime factorization of $|G|$ dictates the existence and sizes
       of its subgroups."
Id: "6c628805-5df1-4ad5-b2c3-23bf7dad5acc"


[\(finite)::group]
Refines: G
implicitly:
specifies: G is \(finite)::monoid
Documented:
. adjective: "finite"
Id: "28d2a1c1-89a5-4163-93e3-fbc21c97ec9a"


Text: "The elements of a group inherit the associative binary operation of the
       ambient structure, and each element $x$ is equipped with its two-sided
       inverse. We denote this inverse by $x^{-1}$, enabling familiar algebraic
       manipulations such as left and right division:
       $x * y = z \iff y = x^{-1} * z$."
Id: "bb323028-2831-4d67-b784-b0918774ab3a"


[\group.element:of{G}]
Declares: x
when: G is \group
extends: x is \monoid.element:of{G}
Enables:
. capability: x.inv :=> \group.inverse:of{x}:in{G}
  written: "x+?^{-1}"
Documented:
. called: "element of group $G?$"
. description: "An element of a group is a member of its underlying set. Because
                the ambient structure is a group, each such element $x$ has a
                well-defined inverse $x^{-1}$, accessible through its inverse
                operation."
Id: "858abdda-c416-4a4e-a0bf-27ecf84cf008"


[\group.inverse:of{x}:in{G ::= (X, *, e)}]
Defines: y "in" G
when:
. G is \group
. x "in" G
expresses:
. x * y = e
. y * x = e
Documented:
. called: "group inverse of $x?$"
. written: "x+?^{-1}"
. description: "The inverse $x^{-1}$ of an element $x$ of a group is the unique
                element $y$ satisfying $x * y = e = y * x$. Its uniqueness is
                proved below; it is what allows every equation in a group to be
                solved."
Id: "9b52b844-0cfa-4238-8c73-8b38265cca81"


Text: "Two elements $x, y$ in a group *commute* when their product is
       independent of order: $x * y = y * x$. In general groups—such as
       permutation groups $S_n$ or matrix groups
       $\operatorname{GL}_n$—multiplication is profoundly non-commutative. The
       degree to which commutativity fails is measured by the *commutator*
       $[x, y] = x * y * x^{-1} * y^{-1}$, which equals the identity $e$ if and
       only if $x$ and $y$ commute."
Id: "a36041ed-5bdd-4e9c-84ce-719a69b7708b"


[x \.commutes.with:?in{M ::= (X, *)}./ y]
States:
when:
. M is \magma
. x, y "in" M
that: x * y = y * x
Documented:
. called: "$x?$ commutes with $y?$@[M]{ in $M?$}"
. description: "Two elements $x$ and $y$ of a magma commute when $x * y = y *
                x$. An operation in which every pair of elements commutes is
                exactly a commutative one."
Id: "3d7a204c-966d-47e7-a5b4-8f9e20fd749c"


SectionTitle: "Subgroups"
Id: "29c1456f-88a5-4b56-a5b8-57859c40aedc"


Text: "Before formalizing subgroups, we establish an elementary set-theoretic
       lemma: the Cartesian product of a subset with itself is a subset of the
       Cartesian square of the ambient set. This lemma guarantees that
       restricting a binary operation $* : X \times X \to X$ to $X' \times X'$
       yields a well-typed mapping into $X$."
Id: "7541e6eb-f20a-497b-ae90-65c891609d26"


[\cross.of.subset.is.subset.of.cross]
Theorem:
given:
. X is \set
. Y \:subset:/ X
then:
. (.Y \.set.cross./ Y.) \:subset?:/ (.X \.set.cross./ X.)
Id: "db0160ae-5d28-4ad1-b2b6-d22964a88a2b"


Text: "A *subgroup* $H \leq G$ is a subset of a group that is itself a group
       under the inherited operation. To qualify as a subgroup, $H$ must be
       nonempty, closed under the group operation ($x * y \in H$), and closed
       under inverses ($x^{-1} \in H$).

       By the *One-Step Subgroup Test*, a nonempty subset $H \subseteq G$ is a
       subgroup if and only if for all $x, y \in H$, the product
       $x * y^{-1} \in H$. Subgroups form the atomic building blocks of group
       theory: every group contains the trivial subgroup $\{e\}$ and the
       improper subgroup $G$, while the intersection of any family of subgroups
       is again a subgroup."
Id: "91933534-107f-44fe-9ad6-4829664f8cd6"


[H ::= (X', *', e') \:subgroup:/ G ::= (X, *, e)]
Declares: H ::= (X', *', e')
when: G is \group
specifies:
. X' \:(nonempty)::subset:/ X
. *' is \binary.operation:on{X'}
. e' "in" X'
satisfies:
. (.*' := \restriction:of{`*`}:on{X' \.set.cross./ X'}.)[:1:]
. have:
  . forAll: x, y "in" H
    then:
    . x *' y "in" H
    . x *' y = x * y
  . forAll: x "in" H
    then: x.inv "in" H
  asserting: H is \group
Enables:
. view:
  as: H' := H is \group
Documented:
. called: "$H?$ is a subgroup of $G?$"
. written: "H? \leq G?"
. description: "A subgroup $H \leq G$ is a nonempty subset of $G$ that is closed
                under the group operation and under taking inverses, and whose
                operation is the restriction of that of $G$. Under these
                conditions $H$ is again a group in its own right."
Justification:
. [1]
  have: *' := \restriction:of{`*`}:on{X' \.set.cross./ X'}
  asserting: (.X' \.set.cross./ X'.) \:subset?:/ (.G \.set.cross./ G.)
  because: X' \:subset?:/ X
  by: \cross.of.subset.is.subset.of.cross#given{X := X; Y := X'}
Id: "d8fe82d3-6f50-4711-ad64-76d4eb0615ff"


Text: "That a subgroup is genuinely a group is recorded as a theorem, making
       explicit the property the definition was arranged to secure."
Id: "5a7d1bd6-3a8d-4584-bc3d-505eb0ba0680"


[\subgroup.is.group]
Theorem:
given:
. G is \group
. H \:subgroup:/ G
then: H is? \group
Id: "946d3180-8614-4bd2-8fd6-d9bdce721e04"


SectionTitle: "Abelian Groups and Inversion Theorems"
Id: "4f086ee9-6118-4df5-903c-f2463f65c0f1"


Text: "When the operation of a group is commutative—meaning $x * y = y * x$ for
       all pairs of elements—the group is called *abelian* (in honor of Niels
       Henrik Abel). In abelian groups, the binary operation is often denoted
       additively by $+$, with identity $0$ and inverse $-x$.

       Abelian groups are far more algebraically tractable than non-commutative
       groups: every subgroup of an abelian group is normal, the set of
       homomorphisms between two abelian groups forms an abelian group, and the
       Fundamental Theorem of Finitely Generated Abelian Groups provides a
       complete, canonical classification into direct sums of cyclic groups."
Id: "a608ecec-59d4-4939-a43e-3296c77eff39"


[\(abelian)::group]
Refines: G ::= (X, *, e)
satisfies:
. forAll: x, y "in" G
  then: x * y = y * x
Documented:
. adjective: "abelian"
Id: "1dc777cd-31a5-489a-8b5a-05b94c647fb9"


Text: "A primary consequence of the group axioms is the rule for inverting a
       compound product: the inverse of a product is the product of the inverses
       in *reverse order*. The reversal is essential in non-abelian groups to
       ensure that adjacent elements cancel."
Id: "a71309b4-a9ab-464d-a885-34cbcf992013"


Theorem:
given:
. G is \group
. x, y "in" G
then: (x * y).inv = y.inv * x.inv
Documented:
. called: "Inverse of a Product"
. description: "In any group, $(x * y)^{-1} = y^{-1} * x^{-1}$."
Proof: """
To prove that $(x * y)^{-1} = y^{-1} * x^{-1}$, by the uniqueness of inverses it suffices
to show that $y^{-1} * x^{-1}$ satisfies both defining inverse equations for $x * y$:
$$ (x * y) * (y^{-1} * x^{-1}) = e \quad \text{and} \quad (y^{-1} * x^{-1}) * (x * y) = e. $$

Applying associativity repeatedly:
$$ (x * y) * (y^{-1} * x^{-1}) = x * (y * y^{-1}) * x^{-1} = x * e * x^{-1} = x * x^{-1} = e. $$
Similarly, multiplying on the left:
$$ (y^{-1} * x^{-1}) * (x * y) = y^{-1} * (x^{-1} * x) * y = y^{-1} * e * y = y^{-1} * y = e. $$

Therefore, $(x * y)^{-1} = y^{-1} * x^{-1}$. The order of factors is reversed:
putting on socks and then shoes is undone by removing shoes first, then socks.
"""
Id: "ef1b2ca8-1e12-4779-8e32-ed5cf8787c68"


Text: "Inverting an element twice returns the original element. In other words,
       the inversion map $\iota : G \to G$ given by $\iota(x) = x^{-1}$ is an
       involution."
Id: "6619e809-8d59-4180-820d-ff40b5914fdf"


Theorem:
given:
. G ::= (X, *, e) is \group
. x "in" G
then: (x.inv).inv = x
Documented:
. called: "Double Inversion Law"
. description: "$(x^{-1})^{-1} = x$ for every element in a group."
Proof: """By definition, $(x^{-1})^{-1}$ is the unique element $z \in G$
          satisfying:
          $$ x^{-1} * z = e \quad \text{and} \quad z * x^{-1} = e. $$ However,
          the element $x$ itself satisfies:
          $$ x^{-1} * x = e \quad \text{and} \quad x * x^{-1} = e. $$ By the
          uniqueness of inverses, it follows that $(x^{-1})^{-1} = x$. Thus
          inversion is an involutive bijection on $G$."""
Id: "8c61fb44-d5ab-4be8-804a-0d6a95d83331"


Text: "Any two elements that both act as an inverse of $x$ must be identical.
       This uniqueness justifies writing $x^{-1}$ for *the* well-defined
       inverse."
Id: "de8e5629-d42e-4d98-b713-62d92e61d306"


Theorem:
given:
. G ::= (X, *, e) is \group
. x, y1, y2 "in" G
where:
. x * y1 = e
. y1 * x = e
. x * y2 = e
. y2 * x = e
then: y1 = y2
Documented:
. called: "Uniqueness of Inverses"
. description: "If $y_1$ and $y_2$ both act as inverses for $x$, then
                $y_1 = y_2$."
Proof: """
Suppose $y_1$ and $y_2$ both satisfy the equations for an inverse of $x$:
$x * y_1 = e = y_1 * x$ and $x * y_2 = e = y_2 * x$.
Using the identity property and associativity:
$$ y_1 = y_1 * e = y_1 * (x * y_2) = (y_1 * x) * y_2 = e * y_2 = y_2. $$
Thus any two elements satisfying the inverse equations must coincide.
"""
Id: "a27fd487-85b4-4f18-b0ab-4be63db69f43"


SectionTitle: "Structural Consequences"
Id: "191bffbb-05de-4543-95d1-5161df7a4c17"


Theorem:
given:
. G is \group
. a "in" G
then:
. \left.cancellable{a}:in{G}
. \right.cancellable{a}:in{G}
Documented:
. called: "Group Cancellation Laws"
. description: "Every group element is both left and right cancellable."
Proof: """
Let $a \in G$. To demonstrate left cancellability, suppose $a * x = a * y$ for $x, y \in G$.
Multiplying both sides on the left by $a^{-1}$ gives:
$$ a^{-1} * (a * x) = a^{-1} * (a * y). $$
By associativity and the definition of inverses:
$$ (a^{-1} * a) * x = (a^{-1} * a) * y \implies e * x = e * y \implies x = y. $$

Similarly, to demonstrate right cancellability, suppose $x * a = y * a$. Multiplying
both sides on the right by $a^{-1}$ gives:
$$ (x * a) * a^{-1} = (y * a) * a^{-1} \implies x * (a * a^{-1}) = y * (a * a^{-1}) \implies x * e = y * e \implies x = y. $$
Thus every element in a group is both left and right cancellable.
"""
Id: "24b48a0a-ec3e-49f2-acd4-80b979fcd209"


TextTheorem: "Lagrange's Theorem. If $G$ is a finite group and $H \leq G$ is a
              subgroup, then the order $|H|$ divides the order $|G|$, and
              $|G| = [G : H] \cdot |H|$, where the index $[G : H]$ is the number
              of distinct left cosets of $H$ in $G$.

              *Proof Sketch*: Define a relation on $G$ by
              $a \sim b \iff a^{-1} * b \in H$. This is an equivalence relation
              whose equivalence classes are the left cosets
              $aH = \{a * h : h \in H\}$. Left cancellation implies that the map
              $h \mapsto a * h$ is a bijection from $H$ onto $aH$, so each coset
              contains precisely $|H|$ elements. Since the equivalence classes
              partition $G$ into $[G : H]$ disjoint subsets each of size $|H|$,
              we conclude $|G| = [G : H] \cdot |H|$.

              *Corollaries*:
              1. The order of every element $g \in G$ divides $|G|$, since the
                 cyclic subgroup $\langle g \rangle$ has order equal to the
                 order of $g$. Consequently, $g^{|G|} = e$ for every $g \in G$.
              2. (Euler-Fermat): In number theory, applying this to the unit
                 group $(\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z})^\times$ of order $\varphi(n)$
                 yields $a^{\varphi(n)} \equiv 1 \pmod n$ for all
                 $\gcd(a, n) = 1$.
              3. Any group of prime order $p$ is cyclic and simple, with no
                 non-trivial subgroups.

              *Remark*: The converse of Lagrange's theorem is false: if $d$
              divides $|G|$, $G$ need not have a subgroup of order $d$. The
              alternating group $A_4$ has order $12$, but possesses no subgroup
              of order $6$."
Id: "08d58b28-0472-45b2-b9f0-49c441c56fd8"


TextTheorem: "Cayley's Theorem. Every group $G$ is isomorphic to a subgroup of the
              symmetric group $\operatorname{Sym}(G)$ of permutations on its underlying carrier set.
              
              *Proof*: For each $g \in G$, define the left translation map
              $\lambda_g : G \to G$ by $\lambda_g(x) = g * x$.
              By the cancellation laws, $\lambda_g$ is injective, and for any $y \in G$,
              $\lambda_g(g^{-1} * y) = y$, so $\lambda_g$ is surjective; hence
              $\lambda_g \in \operatorname{Sym}(G)$.
              Now define the left regular representation $\Lambda : G \to \operatorname{Sym}(G)$
              by $\Lambda(g) = \lambda_g$.
              Associativity ensures that $\Lambda$ is a group homomorphism:
              $\lambda_{g * h}(x) = (g * h) * x = g * (h * x) = (\lambda_g \circ \lambda_h)(x)$,
              so $\Lambda(g * h) = \Lambda(g) \circ \Lambda(h)$.
              Furthermore, $\Lambda$ is injective: if $\lambda_g = \operatorname{id}_G$,
              then $g = g * e = \lambda_g(e) = \operatorname{id}_G(e) = e$, meaning
              $\ker(\Lambda) = \{e\}$.
              
              By the First Isomorphism Theorem, $G \cong \operatorname{im}(\Lambda) \leq \operatorname{Sym}(G)$.
              Thus, abstract axiomatic groups are precisely concrete groups of permutations."
Id: "d9f52cc6-df8a-4e42-8fa7-d06aeb3669db"
